1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:04,800
More than a mile underground, in a maze of tunnels.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:10,940
Workers toil in darkness to unearth one of nature's most precious resources.

3
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:18,060
While above ground, big boys with big toys seek the same prize in a super-sized sandbox.

4
00:00:20,080 --> 00:00:26,720
Pulverized, processed, and poured, our world wouldn't be the same without this gleaming metal.

5
00:00:26,721 --> 00:00:30,720
Now, Silver Minds on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:41,720 --> 00:00:47,720
It's in your car, on your photographs, and around your finger.

7
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:52,720
You eat with it, compute with it, and admire its beauty.

8
00:00:52,721 --> 00:00:59,182
beauty. Silver is one of the most singular and versatile metals known to man.

9
00:00:59,183 --> 00:01:00,860
Silver is an amazing

10
00:01:00,861 --> 00:01:01,561
metal.

11
00:01:01,562 --> 00:01:06,940
It has the highest sheen of any metal and it has the highest conductivity of any metal.

12
00:01:06,941 --> 00:01:12,380
Because it does not corrode, silver can be used in printed circuit boards, in switches,

13
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,860
where things have to work first time every time. That's where you need silver.

14
00:01:16,861 --> 00:01:20,900
And the only way to get silver is to mine it.

15
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:25,920
There are two basic methods for mining silver.

16
00:01:26,140 --> 00:01:35,980
One is underground mining, where we have deep shafts going into the ground and the workings of the mine follow veins.

17
00:01:35,981 --> 00:01:47,500
The other method is open pit mines, where large equipment is used to mine relatively near-surface deposits of silver.

18
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:56,080
Northern Idaho's aptly named Silver Valley has been a mining center since the mid-19th century.

19
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:02,480
Over the decades, the region has produced 1.1 billion ounces of silver.

20
00:02:02,481 --> 00:02:07,980
Located here is the deepest operating silver mine in the United States.

21
00:02:09,180 --> 00:02:12,020
Heckler Mining Company is a 114-year-old mining company.

22
00:02:12,300 --> 00:02:16,840
We're here at the Lucky Friday Mine in Idaho, which is our primary silver-producing mine

23
00:02:16,841 --> 00:02:19,380
and has been operating since the 1940s.

24
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:23,960
Lucky Friday sits in a picturesque tree-studded valley.

25
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:29,920
Its small collection of muted green surface structures barely hints at what lies below,

26
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:31,540
way below.

27
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:42,900
The surface entrance into Lucky Friday extends straight down into the Earth, 6,200 feet, equivalent to 4 1⁄4 Empire State Buildings.

28
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:50,840
Branching out from its cylindrical concrete shaft is a series of horizontal tunnels, or drifts.

29
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:55,400
Three of the drifts extend out one mile from the shaft.

30
00:02:55,401 --> 00:03:00,040
The drips terminate at the currently active ore veins,

31
00:03:00,041 --> 00:03:03,280
where smaller tunnels called stopes branch off.

32
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:07,720
At the end of the stopes, you'll find the iconic figure

33
00:03:07,721 --> 00:03:11,340
that's carved out every inch of Lucky Friday's 20 miles of tunnels,

34
00:03:11,341 --> 00:03:13,220
the miner.

35
00:03:13,221 --> 00:03:17,880
It takes a special person to begin his shift

36
00:03:17,881 --> 00:03:21,200
by going down into a dark place.

37
00:03:21,201 --> 00:03:24,500
This is a real special breed of cat that works underground.

38
00:03:25,400 --> 00:03:30,400
Each day, dozens of miners call this netherworld of tunnels home.

39
00:03:30,401 --> 00:03:34,400
It's an environment that can be summed up in three words.

40
00:03:34,401 --> 00:03:38,400
Dark, wet, and warm.

41
00:03:38,401 --> 00:03:43,400
And be sure to come armed with earplugs, because it's also loud.

42
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:53,400
Despite the extreme conditions, the entire mine has to operate like one giant, smoothly synchronized machine.

43
00:03:53,401 --> 00:03:55,400
Its goal is simple.

44
00:03:55,401 --> 00:04:00,400
To move as much silver ore out of the earth as possible, every day.

45
00:04:00,401 --> 00:04:05,400
And since Lucky Friday's miners get paid not by how many hours they work,

46
00:04:05,401 --> 00:04:07,400
but by how much ore they move,

47
00:04:07,401 --> 00:04:11,400
no one wants to be responsible for slowing down the machine.

48
00:04:13,400 --> 00:04:17,400
Arguably the most critical cog in the mine machine is the elevator hoist.

49
00:04:17,401 --> 00:04:19,363
the elevator hoist.

50
00:04:19,364 --> 00:04:26,800
The hoist runs the entire 6,200 foot length of the shaft and is the only

51
00:04:26,801 --> 00:04:32,664
way workers, supplies and ore can be transported above and below ground.

52
00:04:32,665 --> 00:04:34,700
It's the mine's umbilical

53
00:04:34,701 --> 00:04:35,401
cord.

54
00:04:35,402 --> 00:04:40,220
All workers come underground at the start of shift and they leave at the end of

55
00:04:40,221 --> 00:04:41,372
shift via the shaft.

56
00:04:41,373 --> 00:04:45,060
All of the employees that are going underground will load on the

57
00:04:45,061 --> 00:04:50,440
three decks of one of the cages stacked from bottom to top,

58
00:04:50,441 --> 00:04:52,760
like three floors in an elevator.

59
00:04:52,761 --> 00:04:55,640
There's a hoistman that operates this system.

60
00:04:55,641 --> 00:04:58,900
It's not automatic.

61
00:04:58,901 --> 00:05:02,260
He takes his signals via a bell system.

62
00:05:02,261 --> 00:05:06,580
So the gentleman on the cage will bell to a level.

63
00:05:06,581 --> 00:05:11,540
The hoistman then will operate that elevator to the level

64
00:05:11,541 --> 00:05:13,720
that some of the guys are going to get off on.

65
00:05:15,060 --> 00:05:17,460
The hoistman works in a tightly controlled layer.

66
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:25,980
Dual rotating cylinders indicate where each of the two counterbalanced cages are at any given moment.

67
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:33,720
When carrying workers in the cage, the hoist moves at 1,500 feet per minute.

68
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:38,880
It takes four minutes to reach the 5,900 foot level.

69
00:05:38,881 --> 00:05:45,640
And once underground, the miners stay underground for the duration of their 10-hour shift.

70
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:49,920
So once the men are in the mine at the start of shift,

71
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:53,140
then we limit the travel of personnel on the shaft

72
00:05:53,141 --> 00:05:57,520
so that we can maximize the use of the shaft for getting the ore out of the mine.

73
00:05:58,700 --> 00:06:02,460
The area around the shaft is one of the few illuminated parts of the mine.

74
00:06:03,880 --> 00:06:05,980
Here you'll find each level's nerve center,

75
00:06:05,981 --> 00:06:10,620
a collection of switch boxes, gauges, cables, and lifelines.

76
00:06:13,020 --> 00:06:14,800
Nearby, there's a small break room,

77
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:18,960
plus a fully equipped maintenance shop.

78
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:25,240
This is where machines, brutalized by the mining process,

79
00:06:25,440 --> 00:06:26,500
are serviced and repaired.

80
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,200
One such machine is the man carrier.

81
00:06:31,201 --> 00:06:39,860
With miners on board, the main carrier chugs down the mile-long, 12-foot-wide, 16-foot-high drift.

82
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,300
There are a number of utilities that hang overhead, including ventilation line,

83
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:46,980
that as you're riding in in this equipment,

84
00:06:47,520 --> 00:06:51,900
sometimes there's some head clearance issues that you have to be very careful about.

85
00:06:53,540 --> 00:06:58,020
After a bumpy six-minute ride, the drift terminates at the ore headings.

86
00:06:58,021 --> 00:07:04,720
This is where the miners work, in a maze of dark, constricted corridors overrun by mechanized

87
00:07:04,721 --> 00:07:05,720
beasts.

88
00:07:05,721 --> 00:07:12,140
For the unaccustomed, it may as well be another planet.

89
00:07:12,141 --> 00:07:16,220
Very few areas are lighted with any other light than your cap lamp.

90
00:07:16,221 --> 00:07:22,900
You spend the day conducting some very skilled labor, if you will, in the dark.

91
00:07:22,901 --> 00:07:26,580
There's water in the ground, there's water in the equipment and machines.

92
00:07:26,581 --> 00:07:28,380
It's warm and humid.

93
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:34,060
At a depth of 5,900 feet, the virgin rock temperature is about 120 degrees Fahrenheit.

94
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:39,800
We would like to keep the environment down here at or below about 82 degrees Fahrenheit.

95
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:47,640
We blow air into the work headings, and that helps wick the heat away from an employee's skin and keeps him cool.

96
00:07:49,180 --> 00:07:55,040
In a dark place, teeming with heavy equipment, where detonated explosions are a daily occurrence,

97
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,500
Communication between workers is of prime importance.

98
00:08:00,540 --> 00:08:03,140
Walkie-talkies can't transmit through solid rock,

99
00:08:03,680 --> 00:08:06,840
so miners rely on their headlamps to relay basic information.

100
00:08:07,680 --> 00:08:10,740
If I move my head in a circle, that means come towards me.

101
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:14,200
If I move it straight up and down, it means go away from me.

102
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:16,460
If I move it like this, it means stop.

103
00:08:16,461 --> 00:08:17,540
In the line!

104
00:08:20,740 --> 00:08:24,340
At the end of the narrow stopes, the ore vein is ready for mining.

105
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:29,800
The main vein that we're chasing in this particular heading is this dark vein.

106
00:08:29,940 --> 00:08:34,900
This is the one that contains most of the lead, sulfide material, and the silver.

107
00:08:36,080 --> 00:08:42,320
Silver veins are formed when groundwater washes silver particles and other minerals into open fault lines in the earth.

108
00:08:43,600 --> 00:08:49,140
Under extreme pressure and across millions of years, the particles harden into ore veins.

109
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:53,020
This is why silver is most often found combined with other minerals.

110
00:08:55,040 --> 00:09:01,180
Once processed, each ton of ore from this vein will yield roughly 18 pounds of lead,

111
00:09:01,181 --> 00:09:04,680
two pounds of zinc, and 15 ounces of silver.

112
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:12,540
Like most underground mines, Plucky Friday uses a mining method called the blast, muck, and haul cycle.

113
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:18,360
Every miner's goal is to complete one full cycle during his shift.

114
00:09:18,361 --> 00:09:25,360
Miner Jason Chambers prepares to perform the first phase of the cycle by blasting out a section of the ore vein.

115
00:09:25,361 --> 00:09:34,360
Using what's called a jumbo, a wheel-mounted drill, Jason bores a series of blast holes eight feet deep into the ore base.

116
00:09:34,361 --> 00:09:42,360
Each blast hole is packed with an ammonium nitrate-based explosive called water gel.

117
00:09:42,361 --> 00:09:49,640
The explosives are rigged together with detonation cord, or debt cord, as miners call it.

118
00:09:49,641 --> 00:09:56,280
With the use of delays, the explosives will fire in a carefully planned sequence that

119
00:09:56,281 --> 00:10:02,360
will ensure maximum displacement of the ore. Miners call blasting, breaking around.

120
00:10:02,361 --> 00:10:11,320
This is fused primer. It has a primer on one end and an igniter on the other end.

121
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:16,040
You light this in, and it burns down.

122
00:10:16,041 --> 00:10:18,600
It takes about 10 minutes to get to here.

123
00:10:18,601 --> 00:10:21,760
Once this goes, it hits the debt cord.

124
00:10:21,761 --> 00:10:24,880
Debt cord's instantaneous.

125
00:10:24,881 --> 00:10:28,740
And then the sequence, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

126
00:10:28,741 --> 00:10:31,740
To complete the firing circuit, Jason ties the debt cord

127
00:10:31,741 --> 00:10:35,080
into the fuse line primer.

128
00:10:35,081 --> 00:10:36,120
It's not very pretty.

129
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:39,460
There it is.

130
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:47,080
Although it may seem antiquated, a burning fuse is the most convenient and inexpensive

131
00:10:47,081 --> 00:10:49,200
way to ignite a blast.

132
00:10:49,201 --> 00:10:54,740
The fuse burns one foot every 45 seconds.

133
00:10:54,741 --> 00:11:00,580
That gives Jason 10 minutes to secure the stope and reach a comfortable blast distance.

134
00:11:00,581 --> 00:11:05,200
As a safety precaution, our modern Marvel's crew wasn't allowed near the stope after Jason

135
00:11:05,201 --> 00:11:06,520
lit his fuse.

136
00:11:06,521 --> 00:11:11,980
But our cameras were rolling 200 yards away when his explosives finally detonated.

137
00:11:12,880 --> 00:11:16,580
We're fortunate to have the high-quality underground miners that we have here.

138
00:11:21,720 --> 00:11:27,320
And when they shoot a blast like that, they're breaking a round that's making silver, lead, and zinc for us.

139
00:11:28,380 --> 00:11:30,220
That's the sound of money being made.

140
00:11:30,221 --> 00:11:36,540
When a miner first enters a stope after a blast, he uses a scaling bar to bring down

141
00:11:36,541 --> 00:11:37,800
any loose material.

142
00:11:37,801 --> 00:11:45,160
When a miner drills and blasts and opens up a new foot in a drift or in a tunnel, he

143
00:11:45,161 --> 00:11:49,100
is the first person to ever stand in that location, ever.

144
00:11:49,101 --> 00:11:53,460
It's almost like going to the moon.

145
00:11:53,461 --> 00:11:57,640
After scaling, miners drive four-foot-long expanding rock bolts into the ceiling and

146
00:11:57,641 --> 00:12:02,140
walls, or in mining terms, the back and ribs of the stoke.

147
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:06,840
The bolts compress the rock to help prevent cave-ins.

148
00:12:08,540 --> 00:12:11,260
To bore the holes, as well as drive the bolts,

149
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:14,300
workers use Silver Mining's perennial workhorse,

150
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:17,300
the 95-pound jack-leg drill.

151
00:12:18,820 --> 00:12:21,940
Powered by compressed air, it emits water at the tip

152
00:12:21,941 --> 00:12:23,280
to keep dust at bay.

153
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:27,520
This is a Gardner Denver 83 jack-leg drill.

154
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:31,400
And this is the leg part of the machine.

155
00:12:31,401 --> 00:12:33,880
This is what actually pushes the machine forward.

156
00:12:33,881 --> 00:12:37,600
It's controlled by its corn top valve here.

157
00:12:37,601 --> 00:12:41,520
The more you turn it, the more air is fed to this leg,

158
00:12:41,521 --> 00:12:46,220
and the harder the leg pushes, the further it goes up.

159
00:12:46,221 --> 00:12:49,440
To lessen the jack leg's ear-shattering clamor,

160
00:12:49,441 --> 00:12:53,780
miners retrofit their machines with custom noise mufflers.

161
00:12:53,781 --> 00:12:57,700
Rubber tires on here are actually old tires off of cars.

162
00:12:58,040 --> 00:13:00,440
When you buy a machine, it's bare.

163
00:13:00,540 --> 00:13:02,020
We put these on when we get them.

164
00:13:03,820 --> 00:13:06,820
With the back and ribs of his stove bolted and secured,

165
00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:11,780
Jason now moves in with an LHD, or load-haul dump machine.

166
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:16,320
He uses it to remove the broken ore, or muck as it's now called.

167
00:13:18,380 --> 00:13:21,860
Because LHDs are constantly backing in and out of stoves,

168
00:13:22,500 --> 00:13:23,840
the operator sits sideways,

169
00:13:24,640 --> 00:13:26,740
allowing him to easily see in both directions.

170
00:13:29,820 --> 00:13:33,680
Over several trips, Jason transfers all the muck from his stoke

171
00:13:33,681 --> 00:13:36,080
to a holding area called a muck bay.

172
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:44,480
From here, front-end loaders waste no time transferring the ore to 16-ton trucks.

173
00:13:47,140 --> 00:13:50,680
These low-slung haulers are designed with limited headroom in mind.

174
00:13:51,860 --> 00:13:56,500
Most mites have bigger, but these are about the biggest we can fit down here to 16-ton.

175
00:13:58,340 --> 00:14:03,460
The loaded 16-ton trucks head back down the mile-long drift toward the shaft entrance.

176
00:14:04,080 --> 00:14:07,000
A round trip they'll make an average of 35 times a day.

177
00:14:09,440 --> 00:14:14,260
Near the shaft entrance, the operator dumps his muck load into what's called an ore pocket.

178
00:14:14,261 --> 00:14:24,960
At the top of the ore pocket, there's a grizzly, which is a series of square holes made up of crossed I-beams.

179
00:14:25,140 --> 00:14:29,980
The square openings are sized to the maximum size of the material we want to go down that hole.

180
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:37,560
Muck dumped into the ore pocket falls 70 feet and is fed to a room called the loading pocket.

181
00:14:37,561 --> 00:14:47,240
Here, a worker known as a cager fills a bucket, or skip, with 10-ton loads of ore.

182
00:14:47,241 --> 00:14:50,320
The skip is actually the top level of the elevator hoist.

183
00:14:50,321 --> 00:14:55,240
The material is loaded off the belt into what's called a load cell.

184
00:14:55,241 --> 00:14:59,700
The material or ore falls out of the loading cell into the skip, and then he bells the

185
00:14:59,701 --> 00:15:02,880
skip away and the hoistman takes it to the surface.

186
00:15:02,881 --> 00:15:07,880
The hoist hauls 1,000 tons of ore out of Lucky Friday every day.

187
00:15:07,881 --> 00:15:12,880
It's the final leg of the blast muck and haul cycle.

188
00:15:12,881 --> 00:15:15,880
But it's not the end of the line for the ore.

189
00:15:15,881 --> 00:15:21,880
It must now undergo processing to unlock the valuable metals trapped inside.

190
00:15:21,881 --> 00:15:26,880
Lucky Friday has no mine-wide communication system,

191
00:15:26,881 --> 00:15:30,880
so a stench-producing chemical is poured into the ventilation ducts

192
00:15:30,881 --> 00:15:33,640
to alert miners of an emergency evacuation.

193
00:15:36,620 --> 00:15:39,260
The blast muck and hole method used in underground mines

194
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:42,060
like Idaho's Lucky Friday culminates

195
00:15:42,160 --> 00:15:43,700
with the ore being hauled to the surface.

196
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:50,860
But mining and hauling is only half the battle.

197
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:53,500
Now begins the process of extracting the silver and other

198
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:55,780
metals from the rock.

199
00:15:55,880 --> 00:15:59,280
And it all begins in the crushing plant.

200
00:15:59,281 --> 00:16:00,420
This is the crusher.

201
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:02,900
What we do is we crush the rock.

202
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,400
It comes from the feeders, and it goes through all the belts.

203
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:08,560
It's crushed.

204
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:11,520
There's the jaw crusher, the cone crusher,

205
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:13,700
and then we have the fine crusher up back there.

206
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:18,120
If it doesn't go through the screen and drop down into the fine ore bin,

207
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:20,820
it'll keep coming through and get crushed until it's small enough

208
00:16:20,821 --> 00:16:23,780
to fall through the screens and fill up the bin back there.

209
00:16:24,340 --> 00:16:26,200
And then from there, it goes down into the ball mill.

210
00:16:26,201 --> 00:16:32,540
At the ball mill, water is added to the crushed ore, which is then tumbled with thousands

211
00:16:32,541 --> 00:16:36,640
of three-inch diameter steel balls inside a giant rotating drum.

212
00:16:39,260 --> 00:16:43,940
The balls pulverize the zinc, lead, and silver into a soupy slurry mixture.

213
00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:48,620
The slurry is pumped into the flotation cells.

214
00:16:49,560 --> 00:16:52,280
These are tanks where the metals are separated from the slurry.

215
00:16:52,281 --> 00:17:00,200
In each cell, a frother makes bubbles, while an added chemical causes the zinc in one cell

216
00:17:00,201 --> 00:17:05,900
and the lead and silver in another to stick to the bubbles as they rise.

217
00:17:06,340 --> 00:17:09,000
Here, you're actually seeing lead float.

218
00:17:10,460 --> 00:17:16,220
This material runs 70% lead and 120 ounces per ton of silver.

219
00:17:16,221 --> 00:17:23,800
Both the lead silver concentrate and the zinc concentrate are drained from the flotation

220
00:17:23,801 --> 00:17:31,000
cells and piped into separate thickener tanks where most of the water is removed.

221
00:17:31,001 --> 00:17:35,660
Once the concentrate leaves the thickener tank, that material is pumped to these vacuum

222
00:17:35,661 --> 00:17:41,580
filters where the moisture is sucked out of the concentrate through the filter cloth into

223
00:17:41,581 --> 00:17:43,026
the inside of the drum.

224
00:17:43,027 --> 00:17:47,740
The final product then is dry and you can see it coming off of this filter.

225
00:17:49,740 --> 00:17:54,940
The dry silver lead concentrate falls into collection bays in Lucky Friday's loadout facility.

226
00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:02,700
Here, a worker fills trucks with an average of 140 tons of the concentrate every day.

227
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:08,780
It's then hauled to a refinery in Canada for further separation and purification.

228
00:18:08,781 --> 00:18:12,320
This is the end of the process at Lucky Friday.

229
00:18:13,880 --> 00:18:18,520
A process that began with a miner reporting for work at the end of a long, narrow stone.

230
00:18:19,980 --> 00:18:21,460
It's a wonderful place.

231
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:26,480
My only description for it is, any day underground is a good day.

232
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:34,880
Almost all modern underground mining techniques can trace their origins to the most famous silver mining venture in history.

233
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:37,520
The Comstock Load.

234
00:18:37,521 --> 00:18:41,740
a massive silver ore vein discovered in the mountains of western Nevada.

235
00:18:42,520 --> 00:18:49,940
From 1860 through 1880, Nevada's Comstock load was just,

236
00:18:50,500 --> 00:18:53,600
it was the largest producing silver mine in the world.

237
00:18:54,160 --> 00:18:58,460
Produced $300 million worth of silver.

238
00:18:58,461 --> 00:19:02,460
You almost can't overstate how important the Comstock was.

239
00:19:04,440 --> 00:19:07,000
When word of the massive strike first spread,

240
00:19:07,520 --> 00:19:11,620
Thousands of silver seekers poured into the boomtown of Virginia City, Nevada.

241
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:18,420
The Comstock load itself took its name from one of Virginia City's most colorful characters,

242
00:19:18,940 --> 00:19:23,640
a drifter, fur trapper, and sometime miner named Henry Comstock.

243
00:19:24,760 --> 00:19:28,920
Henry Comstock was a shiftless individual, to tell you the truth.

244
00:19:29,100 --> 00:19:30,680
He was always looking to make a buck.

245
00:19:30,800 --> 00:19:35,860
In June of 1859, two Irishmen, Peter O'Reilly and Patrick McLaughlin,

246
00:19:35,861 --> 00:19:37,440
were on the north end of the load here.

247
00:19:37,441 --> 00:19:40,840
When Henry Comstock came by and told the two men

248
00:19:40,841 --> 00:19:43,100
that they were digging on his property,

249
00:19:43,101 --> 00:19:46,600
which was an outright lie, and he didn't own the property.

250
00:19:46,601 --> 00:19:48,900
So they had a real quick little conference there,

251
00:19:48,901 --> 00:19:50,680
and they thought that two thirds of something

252
00:19:50,681 --> 00:19:52,020
was better than 100% or nothing,

253
00:19:52,021 --> 00:19:54,580
so they cut him in for a one third share.

254
00:19:54,581 --> 00:19:57,460
And Comstock was a braggart, and he was telling everybody

255
00:19:57,461 --> 00:19:59,540
that it was my strike, my load.

256
00:19:59,541 --> 00:20:02,000
And pretty soon the people from down below were saying,

257
00:20:02,001 --> 00:20:04,140
let's go up to the Comstock load in Virginia City,

258
00:20:04,141 --> 00:20:05,540
and that's how it got its name.

259
00:20:07,620 --> 00:20:10,560
When the miners sank their first underground shafts,

260
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:13,320
they had to hack through crumbling, unstable earth,

261
00:20:13,700 --> 00:20:15,440
which frequently collapsed on top of them.

262
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:22,220
In 1859, a half-dozen miners died at the Ofer mine,

263
00:20:22,300 --> 00:20:23,780
which is in the north end of the load.

264
00:20:24,320 --> 00:20:26,660
That was followed immediately by the total collapse

265
00:20:26,661 --> 00:20:28,500
of the Mexican mine just north of it,

266
00:20:28,620 --> 00:20:31,920
caving from the 300-foot level all the way to the surface.

267
00:20:31,921 --> 00:20:36,660
It became obvious then that they would have to either improve their timbering methods

268
00:20:36,661 --> 00:20:38,480
or they would not be able to mine here.

269
00:20:39,860 --> 00:20:43,780
Desperate for help, the Comstock workers called in Philipp Dietersheimer,

270
00:20:44,420 --> 00:20:48,160
a young graduate of Germany's prestigious Freiburg School of Mines.

271
00:20:48,940 --> 00:20:56,700
One of the stories I've heard is that he was sitting up on the surface wondering what to do

272
00:20:56,701 --> 00:21:02,040
and noticed the bees coming to the sagebrush

273
00:21:02,041 --> 00:21:06,240
and recognized in a sudden flash of intuition

274
00:21:06,241 --> 00:21:09,900
that the honeycomb could provide an answer.

275
00:21:12,300 --> 00:21:15,360
Dieter Scheimer's timber honeycomb was called the square set,

276
00:21:15,840 --> 00:21:18,280
a simple wooden cube made of massive timbers.

277
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:23,420
Like a honeycomb, each cube interlocked with other cubes.

278
00:21:24,420 --> 00:21:27,140
The interlocked cubes supported each other in layers,

279
00:21:27,700 --> 00:21:29,320
with floors and ceilings in between,

280
00:21:29,780 --> 00:21:32,620
like a giant underground building, or beehive.

281
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:38,020
Basically, they're interlocking square cells of timber.

282
00:21:38,220 --> 00:21:39,560
They're notched to fit together.

283
00:21:40,240 --> 00:21:41,100
You see here.

284
00:21:42,060 --> 00:21:44,280
Because they form a cube or a box,

285
00:21:44,360 --> 00:21:47,560
they can withstand pressure equally from all six directions.

286
00:21:47,820 --> 00:21:49,860
There are no nails used in the construction.

287
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,400
The pressure of the ground holds the unit together.

288
00:21:52,401 --> 00:21:56,480
If we were to remove the rock from around the timber, this would all fall apart.

289
00:21:57,340 --> 00:22:00,720
Kept safe by the square sets and working by candlelight,

290
00:22:01,320 --> 00:22:05,840
the Comstock miners bored holes in the ore using sledgehammers and handheld drills.

291
00:22:07,800 --> 00:22:11,680
Similar to how it's done today, they filled the blast holes with dynamite,

292
00:22:12,220 --> 00:22:14,180
lit a fuse, and ran for cover.

293
00:22:15,800 --> 00:22:18,940
The work was brutal, the men fearless.

294
00:22:18,941 --> 00:22:26,920
They were men who were very proud of their profession, and they were well paid.

295
00:22:27,040 --> 00:22:29,200
They were the highest paid miners in the world here.

296
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:34,140
They were paid $4 a day, which was quite good in the 1860s and 70s.

297
00:22:35,080 --> 00:22:38,080
They were proud to be miners, and they were pretty tough guys.

298
00:22:39,380 --> 00:22:41,440
Toughness was mandatory for this job.

299
00:22:42,460 --> 00:22:47,220
At 2,000 feet below ground, tools were too hot to handle without gloves.

300
00:22:48,220 --> 00:22:52,700
Miners could work only a few minutes out of every hour before the heat exhausted them.

301
00:22:53,780 --> 00:23:00,380
They rested in special cooling rooms, where they drank cold water and sucked ice gathered from nearby mountain peaks.

302
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:04,620
Keeping the Comstock miners comfortable was only half the battle.

303
00:23:06,320 --> 00:23:09,760
Keeping them alive continued to provide its own set of challenges.

304
00:23:09,761 --> 00:23:18,380
Despite the invention of the square set, working deep underground remained a dangerous and deadly pursuit.

305
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:25,680
Henry Comstock sold his claim in the Ophir silver mine for $11,000.

306
00:23:26,500 --> 00:23:34,840
The Ophir ended up yielding $20 million in silver, and the impoverished Comstock took his own life in 1870.

307
00:23:34,841 --> 00:23:42,660
At the base of a tree-lined mountain in Idaho's Silver Valley

308
00:23:42,661 --> 00:23:46,280
stands a grim reminder of the dangers faced by every miner.

309
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:53,040
A monument to the 91 men who perished in the Sunshine Silver Mine fire in 1972.

310
00:23:55,180 --> 00:24:02,540
At the time, the Sunshine Mine had a very large number of stopes

311
00:24:02,541 --> 00:24:06,140
that had a lot of timber in those stoves.

312
00:24:07,140 --> 00:24:10,660
Somehow, a fire started in the old workings

313
00:24:10,661 --> 00:24:11,880
that were not accessible,

314
00:24:12,840 --> 00:24:16,440
and a large number of miners perished

315
00:24:16,441 --> 00:24:20,000
in that fire from carbon monoxide poisoning mainly.

316
00:24:21,360 --> 00:24:24,580
The tragedy led to a host of new safety regulations,

317
00:24:24,980 --> 00:24:28,060
including a law that requires every underground mine

318
00:24:28,061 --> 00:24:31,500
to provide workers with a device called a self-rescuer.

319
00:24:32,540 --> 00:24:34,940
It works kind of like a snorkel.

320
00:24:36,040 --> 00:24:38,120
What you'll do is take your hard hat off

321
00:24:38,121 --> 00:24:41,900
and bite down on these lugs,

322
00:24:42,460 --> 00:24:44,560
just like a snorkel would be.

323
00:24:47,060 --> 00:24:49,740
A self-rescuer uses a chemical process

324
00:24:49,741 --> 00:24:51,580
to convert deadly carbon monoxide

325
00:24:51,581 --> 00:24:53,720
to breathable carbon dioxide.

326
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:56,840
It's effective for about an hour.

327
00:24:56,841 --> 00:25:10,260
improvements in safety over the last decades has been incredible you go to any mine and

328
00:25:10,261 --> 00:25:15,580
there is an ethic today that safety is the most important job on the property

329
00:25:15,581 --> 00:25:23,220
but things were very different back in the comstock days when safety took a back seat to productivity

330
00:25:23,221 --> 00:25:33,460
One infamous example was the invention of the steam-powered drill, later replaced by the

331
00:25:33,461 --> 00:25:37,920
compressed air-powered drill.

332
00:25:37,921 --> 00:25:43,100
The efficient new device enabled miners to work much faster and with less exertion.

333
00:25:43,101 --> 00:25:47,200
It also allowed mine owners to employ fewer workers.

334
00:25:47,201 --> 00:25:51,200
Those workers, however, discovered the disturbing downside of the device.

335
00:25:51,201 --> 00:25:59,800
Because it penetrated rock, it produced a fine dust that ravaged their lungs when inhaled.

336
00:25:59,801 --> 00:26:06,720
Hundreds died of a chronic respiratory illness that came to be called miner's consumption.

337
00:26:06,721 --> 00:26:13,540
In today's mines, the high-powered drills emit water from their tips.

338
00:26:13,541 --> 00:26:19,220
The water suppresses the dangerous rock dust and keeps it from becoming airborne.

339
00:26:19,221 --> 00:26:25,000
In addition to miners' consumption, the Comstock laborers also risked severe burns and even

340
00:26:25,001 --> 00:26:27,880
lethal scalding.

341
00:26:27,881 --> 00:26:33,220
As the drills bored into ancient volcanic rock, they struck springs as hot as 170 degrees

342
00:26:33,221 --> 00:26:36,380
Fahrenheit.

343
00:26:36,381 --> 00:26:38,720
Tunnels often flooded with the scalding water.

344
00:26:38,721 --> 00:26:45,380
There's all kinds of stories about people coming, having bad experiences with that hot

345
00:26:45,381 --> 00:26:46,720
water.

346
00:26:46,721 --> 00:26:59,320
I've heard a man fell in, and even though they pulled him out right away, he was so burned and so boiled that his skin was destroyed, and he didn't live another day.

347
00:27:02,660 --> 00:27:09,340
Using a newly built railroad, the Comstock mines imported huge steam engines powerful enough to pump the tunnels dry.

348
00:27:12,580 --> 00:27:15,560
These pumps were some of the biggest steam engines in the world.

349
00:27:16,720 --> 00:27:23,280
The largest pumps had massive cylinders, 8 feet 4 inches in diameter, that weighed 43 tons.

350
00:27:24,560 --> 00:27:30,700
These cylinders turned a cast iron flywheel weighing 110 tons and measuring 40 feet across.

351
00:27:32,820 --> 00:27:38,920
The enormous flywheel drove a pump rod that plunged 3,000 feet into the earth, over half a mile.

352
00:27:38,921 --> 00:27:46,600
Rising and falling 10 times a minute, the pump rod could lift 2 million gallons of water

353
00:27:46,601 --> 00:27:52,100
from a mine in a single day.

354
00:27:52,101 --> 00:27:56,820
By the early 1880s, the steam-powered hoists had hauled just about all that the Comstock

355
00:27:56,821 --> 00:27:59,880
mines had to offer.

356
00:27:59,881 --> 00:28:05,880
After extracting more than 7 million tons of silver ore, worth more than $300 million,

357
00:28:05,881 --> 00:28:07,820
equivalent to $5 billion today.

358
00:28:08,980 --> 00:28:12,040
The boom days of the Comstock Load were coming to an end.

359
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:20,300
The miners and dreamers began leaving the area for new silver strikes

360
00:28:20,301 --> 00:28:23,640
in Tombstone, Arizona, and Idaho's Silver Valley.

361
00:28:24,760 --> 00:28:29,300
They left behind hundreds of their comrades who died mining the Comstock Load.

362
00:28:30,040 --> 00:28:31,740
Some in the Virginia City Cemetery.

363
00:28:31,741 --> 00:28:35,900
others in the 750 miles of shafts and tunnels.

364
00:28:38,540 --> 00:28:40,280
But in decades to come,

365
00:28:40,700 --> 00:28:43,320
the impact of their pioneering efforts would endure.

366
00:28:45,200 --> 00:28:49,000
The Comstock's legacy has been certainly a lot of innovation

367
00:28:49,001 --> 00:28:50,960
for modern mining.

368
00:28:51,220 --> 00:28:55,240
We've adopted many of the techniques that were developed in the Comstock,

369
00:28:55,760 --> 00:28:57,480
ideas about ground control,

370
00:28:57,620 --> 00:29:00,620
that is how do you support the ground around you,

371
00:29:00,621 --> 00:29:04,620
New ideas came out of the Comstock load.

372
00:29:06,620 --> 00:29:10,620
Today, silver is still being mined near Virginia City,

373
00:29:10,621 --> 00:29:15,620
but with tools and techniques unimaginable to the pioneers of the past.

374
00:29:21,620 --> 00:29:25,620
The 18th century phrase, born with a silver spoon in your mouth,

375
00:29:25,621 --> 00:29:27,620
refers to health as well as wealth.

376
00:29:27,621 --> 00:29:34,440
wealth, silver possesses antibacterial properties, and babies fed with silver spoons stayed healthier

377
00:29:34,441 --> 00:29:38,300
than those fed with spoons made from other metals.

378
00:29:38,301 --> 00:29:47,400
When a rich vein of silver ore is discovered deep inside the earth, costly underground

379
00:29:47,401 --> 00:29:51,840
mining is the only option for extracting it.

380
00:29:51,841 --> 00:29:57,440
When an ore deposit is situated close to the surface and has little overburden or topsoil,

381
00:29:57,441 --> 00:30:02,440
open pit mining is the most effective method.

382
00:30:02,441 --> 00:30:05,440
Although the goal of both methods is the same,

383
00:30:05,441 --> 00:30:12,440
the techniques, equipment, and processes are as different as night and day.

384
00:30:12,441 --> 00:30:16,440
Open pit mining requires a significant footprint.

385
00:30:16,441 --> 00:30:20,440
It does require a lot of heavy equipment.

386
00:30:20,441 --> 00:30:26,440
Trucks that have capacities of over 100 tons in most cases.

387
00:30:26,441 --> 00:30:32,580
Large dozers that can push with a dozer blade 14 feet wide.

388
00:30:32,581 --> 00:30:37,960
This is stuff that you don't see in your backyard every day, that's for sure.

389
00:30:39,340 --> 00:30:44,120
Not unless your backyard happens to be the Rochester pit mine near Lovelock, Nevada.

390
00:30:46,060 --> 00:30:51,740
Operated by Coeur d'Alene Mining, Rochester is the largest primary silver mine in the United States.

391
00:30:51,741 --> 00:30:56,900
Although its ore contains other valuable metals like gold and mercury,

392
00:30:56,901 --> 00:31:00,100
the mine's primary revenue is derived from silver.

393
00:31:00,101 --> 00:31:06,980
This is the only primary silver producing mine in the state of Nevada at this time.

394
00:31:06,981 --> 00:31:12,920
This operates 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, 365 days a year.

395
00:31:13,500 --> 00:31:16,460
The Rochester mine began production in 1986.

396
00:31:16,461 --> 00:31:22,640
and since that time we have produced 111 million ounces of silver.

397
00:31:23,240 --> 00:31:28,460
If you were to take 111 million ounces of silver in a silver dollar shape

398
00:31:28,461 --> 00:31:33,920
and stack it up, it would go 220 miles into the air.

399
00:31:34,380 --> 00:31:37,640
That is how much silver we have produced here at Cool Rockchester.

400
00:31:38,920 --> 00:31:42,760
Open pit mines are carved out of the earth in giant terraced levels.

401
00:31:42,761 --> 00:31:51,880
Like underground mining, open pit mining exposes the ore by blasting, but its detonations

402
00:31:51,881 --> 00:31:53,520
pack a far greater wallop.

403
00:31:55,180 --> 00:32:00,020
Five, four, three, two, one, fire and hold.

404
00:32:03,020 --> 00:32:08,180
In less than a second, the blast creates a 75,000-ton pile of ore.

405
00:32:09,500 --> 00:32:10,220
Shot fired.

406
00:32:10,320 --> 00:32:11,320
Please stand by for the all clear.

407
00:32:12,200 --> 00:32:17,780
The grade in an open pit mine is generally less than what we had seen in an underground mine.

408
00:32:18,220 --> 00:32:20,900
That means, though, that we have to move a lot more tons

409
00:32:20,901 --> 00:32:24,780
to get the same number of ounces as you would in an underground mine.

410
00:32:27,840 --> 00:32:31,860
After blasting, an assaying team tests the ore for silver content.

411
00:32:33,060 --> 00:32:37,680
They then produce a computer model that shows where the higher and lower grade ores are located.

412
00:32:41,320 --> 00:32:48,320
Around the clock, 100-ton capacity trucks wind their way out of the pit.

413
00:32:48,321 --> 00:32:56,320
The trucks hauling the highest grade ore dump their loads here, into the insatiable maw of

414
00:32:56,321 --> 00:32:59,320
Rochester's massive primary crusher.

415
00:32:59,321 --> 00:33:07,320
The mouth of the crusher measures four by five feet and can swallow 100-ton loads littered

416
00:33:07,321 --> 00:33:10,320
with boulder-sized rocks.

417
00:33:10,321 --> 00:33:17,320
Inside are two jaws, one movable, the other stationary.

418
00:33:17,321 --> 00:33:23,320
The movable jaw's short, rapid motion crushes the ore, chewing it up like an animal.

419
00:33:23,321 --> 00:33:31,320
The jaw is driven by a 500-horsepower electric motor that propels two massive 6,500-pound flywheels.

420
00:33:31,321 --> 00:33:40,640
wheels. The ore exiting the primary crusher travels along a conveyor belt on its way

421
00:33:40,641 --> 00:33:42,899
through two more crushers.

422
00:33:42,900 --> 00:33:48,200
Finally, it emerges at the end, no bigger than 3 eighths inch

423
00:33:48,201 --> 00:33:49,413
sized gravel.

424
00:33:49,414 --> 00:33:56,320
While all this is happening, the lower grade ore bypasses all the crushers

425
00:33:56,321 --> 00:33:57,720
in order to save time and money.

426
00:33:59,040 --> 00:34:01,040
Containing small amounts of valuable silver,

427
00:34:01,520 --> 00:34:04,660
it's still worth processing using the same method as the crushed ore.

428
00:34:06,400 --> 00:34:09,900
Because Rochester produces 50,000 tons of ore a day,

429
00:34:10,600 --> 00:34:14,700
a bulk processing method called cyanide heat leaching is used.

430
00:34:15,620 --> 00:34:19,920
It all happens on an enormous apparatus covering hundreds of acres

431
00:34:19,921 --> 00:34:21,500
called a leach pad.

432
00:34:21,501 --> 00:34:28,660
A leach pad starts as a giant plastic liner placed on inclined, compacted clay.

433
00:34:30,620 --> 00:34:33,960
A 20-foot-deep layer of ore is then dumped onto the pad.

434
00:34:36,200 --> 00:34:39,680
Workers lay out rows of plastic drip tubing across the ore.

435
00:34:42,120 --> 00:34:46,640
A dilute cyanide solution pumped through the tubing percolates into the ore.

436
00:34:46,641 --> 00:34:54,740
Cyanide leeches, or extracts, silver, gold, and other metals from the rock, like water-dissolved salt.

437
00:34:56,000 --> 00:35:02,400
This so-called pregnant solution collects in a dike at the bottom of the pad, where it's continuously pumped out.

438
00:35:03,620 --> 00:35:09,440
Additional layers of ore and tubing are applied to the pad until it plunges to depths of 300 feet or more.

439
00:35:09,441 --> 00:35:15,220
Rochester has two leech bags.

440
00:35:15,580 --> 00:35:20,660
The first covers 134 acres and is currently 300 feet deep.

441
00:35:21,300 --> 00:35:24,320
It holds the lowest grade ore, called run-of-mine ore,

442
00:35:24,680 --> 00:35:26,060
which has bypassed the crusher.

443
00:35:27,340 --> 00:35:28,800
The guys are running out our drip tube.

444
00:35:29,140 --> 00:35:32,580
They will run it out over the top of this run-of-mine material by hand.

445
00:35:32,940 --> 00:35:35,920
They run it out to the header line where we hook it into the nipples

446
00:35:35,921 --> 00:35:38,800
and put solution to it and let it run.

447
00:35:39,440 --> 00:35:44,440
Rochester's second leach pad holds the crushed high-grade ore.

448
00:35:44,441 --> 00:35:49,440
A tractor buries multiple drip tube lines a few inches under the ore.

449
00:35:49,441 --> 00:35:54,440
Allowing for less evaporation, it's a more efficient way to deliver the cyanide solution,

450
00:35:54,441 --> 00:35:58,440
but impossible to do on the rocky low-grade leach pad.

451
00:35:58,441 --> 00:36:02,440
Next stop, the processing plant.

452
00:36:02,441 --> 00:36:08,440
Entering the plant through a large pipe, the pregnant solution is circulated through these filter tanks.

453
00:36:08,441 --> 00:36:11,734
tanks, the same kind used in winemaking.

454
00:36:11,735 --> 00:36:15,440
The solution then travels to the deaerator, a

455
00:36:16,820 --> 00:36:19,971
vacuum tower that sucks out most of the oxygen.

456
00:36:19,972 --> 00:36:22,920
This is done to prepare the solution for its

457
00:36:22,921 --> 00:36:27,587
next phase of processing, in which powdered zinc is added.

458
00:36:27,588 --> 00:36:29,920
Zinc has an ionic charge that

459
00:36:31,200 --> 00:36:35,495
enables it to efficiently attract dissolved silver and gold.

460
00:36:35,496 --> 00:36:37,500
If oxygen were present, this

461
00:36:37,501 --> 00:36:39,560
traction would be decreased.

462
00:36:39,561 --> 00:36:42,100
The solution is then sent to banks of filter presses.

463
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:47,840
It's here that the liquid is separated from the solids,

464
00:36:47,841 --> 00:36:51,540
leaving a muddy mixture of mostly silver called filter

465
00:36:51,541 --> 00:36:52,440
cake.

466
00:36:52,441 --> 00:36:55,380
When we clean the filters, this is why it comes out.

467
00:36:55,381 --> 00:36:59,180
We take this material, we load it into trays,

468
00:36:59,181 --> 00:37:03,660
we place it into a retort, which is basically a big oven.

469
00:37:03,661 --> 00:37:07,500
We remove the mercury, which is a byproduct for us.

470
00:37:07,501 --> 00:37:12,500
The material that comes out of the retort looks like this.

471
00:37:12,600 --> 00:37:16,840
This is what we put into the furnace.

472
00:37:16,940 --> 00:37:18,440
The furnace heats the filter cake

473
00:37:18,540 --> 00:37:22,740
until it melts at about 2,200 degrees Fahrenheit.

474
00:37:22,840 --> 00:37:24,660
Then it's time to pour it into molds.

475
00:37:27,860 --> 00:37:30,560
In this state, the silver is now called dore.

476
00:37:30,561 --> 00:37:41,040
After a few minutes, the dore is cool enough to remove from the mold.

477
00:37:41,041 --> 00:37:45,580
Workers accomplish the tricky maneuver with a forklift.

478
00:37:45,581 --> 00:37:52,420
This is the Rochester mine's final product, a 300-pound dore bar that is 98% silver with

479
00:37:52,421 --> 00:37:54,820
trace elements of gold.

480
00:37:54,821 --> 00:37:59,260
It will be sold and shipped to a refinery, where the silver and gold will be separated

481
00:37:59,261 --> 00:38:08,940
and purify each of the doré bars is worth about 40 to 50 thousand dollars at today's prices

482
00:38:08,941 --> 00:38:15,500
my general manager always says if you can pick up one of these bars which raise what roughly 300

483
00:38:15,501 --> 00:38:23,020
pounds you can have it but uh i say no way one day someone's gonna come here and carry one off and

484
00:38:23,021 --> 00:38:26,820
and I'll be $50,000 in the hole.

485
00:38:27,020 --> 00:38:29,960
Five, four, three, two, three, two, three,

486
00:38:29,961 --> 00:38:33,640
which begins with a blast and ends with a bar,

487
00:38:34,580 --> 00:38:36,360
is in its final days at Rochester.

488
00:38:37,060 --> 00:38:40,500
The pit is scheduled to close in December 2006.

489
00:38:41,220 --> 00:38:44,380
The processing plant will be shut down five years later.

490
00:38:45,400 --> 00:38:49,220
Then, a whole new chapter in the life of the mine will begin,

491
00:38:49,920 --> 00:38:52,940
one which will return it, and the land, to nature.

492
00:38:53,020 --> 00:39:01,120
The largest silver nugget ever found in the United States was extracted from Colorado's

493
00:39:01,121 --> 00:39:08,764
smuggler mine in 1894. It weighed 1840 pounds and was worth about $35,000.

494
00:39:08,765 --> 00:39:10,520
At today's silver

495
00:39:10,521 --> 00:39:13,789
prices, that's more than $300,000.

496
00:39:13,790 --> 00:39:18,980
Silver mines supply a vital material to today's world.

497
00:39:18,981 --> 00:39:26,660
By their very nature, however, mines can't help but affect the land, air, and water systems

498
00:39:26,661 --> 00:39:27,660
that surround them.

499
00:39:27,661 --> 00:39:32,220
Almost everything we do has an impact on the earth and Mother Nature, and mining's

500
00:39:32,221 --> 00:39:34,440
no different than anything else we do.

501
00:39:34,441 --> 00:39:39,480
So one of the major goals of any mining operation is to have the minimum impact that it can

502
00:39:39,481 --> 00:39:42,600
on the environment.

503
00:39:42,601 --> 00:39:47,100
Most of the environmental regulations that silver mines currently operate under are results

504
00:39:47,101 --> 00:39:49,040
of the Clean Air Act of 1970.

505
00:39:50,600 --> 00:39:54,140
Today, mining has to pay attention to the environment.

506
00:39:54,400 --> 00:39:58,760
It's far different than it was even when I started in the business 30 years ago.

507
00:39:59,140 --> 00:40:04,860
We simply cannot do the types of activities that we did 30 years ago

508
00:40:04,861 --> 00:40:07,060
without thinking about the environment.

509
00:40:09,200 --> 00:40:11,320
Back in the days of the Comstock load,

510
00:40:11,660 --> 00:40:13,980
there were no such things as environmental regulations.

511
00:40:13,981 --> 00:40:20,160
The miners took whatever they needed from the environment and poured pollutants like

512
00:40:20,161 --> 00:40:24,120
mercury and lead back into it.

513
00:40:24,121 --> 00:40:29,700
To build square sets to support Comstock mines, 80 million board feet of lumber was required

514
00:40:29,701 --> 00:40:33,080
every year.

515
00:40:33,081 --> 00:40:39,600
The miners used enough lumber to build 27,000 two-bedroom homes and in the process stripped

516
00:40:39,601 --> 00:40:43,820
the hillsides around Virginia City bare.

517
00:40:43,821 --> 00:40:49,380
In comparison, not far from Virginia City, at the Rochester open pit mine, the Coeur

518
00:40:49,381 --> 00:40:55,500
de Laine mining company was required to post a $31.6 million bond to fund reclamation of

519
00:40:55,501 --> 00:40:57,880
the land.

520
00:40:57,881 --> 00:41:02,780
Although the open pit will remain, all the structures and leach pads will be removed

521
00:41:02,781 --> 00:41:07,120
and the land revegetated.

522
00:41:07,121 --> 00:41:12,940
This 85-acre section of hillside used to be one of Rochester's primary leach pads.

523
00:41:12,941 --> 00:41:17,020
We shut it down in 1999 and commenced with closure.

524
00:41:17,320 --> 00:41:21,320
That began by treating the rock that's on top of the plastic liner

525
00:41:21,321 --> 00:41:25,500
and making sure to neutralize the chemicals that remain.

526
00:41:26,120 --> 00:41:28,960
We placed topsoil on top of that

527
00:41:28,961 --> 00:41:34,340
and planted it with native seed mix approved by the regulatory agencies.

528
00:41:34,341 --> 00:41:36,740
The native shrubs are coming up very nicely,

529
00:41:37,080 --> 00:41:39,440
and you can't even hardly tell that it's here.

530
00:41:39,441 --> 00:41:45,100
Mining the land no longer means having to leave a permanent scar.

531
00:41:48,840 --> 00:41:54,580
In 2005, the Heckla Mining Company received Nevada's Excellence in Reclamation Award

532
00:41:54,581 --> 00:41:57,060
for its cleanup work at the Rosebud Mine.

533
00:41:57,860 --> 00:42:02,460
After the mine closed in 2000, Heckla and its partner, Newmont Mining,

534
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:06,260
removed buildings, then recontoured and revegetated the property.

535
00:42:06,261 --> 00:42:12,380
I'm really proud to be involved with an industry that has made such great strides that it has

536
00:42:12,381 --> 00:42:17,960
over the last 20 years, and certainly within the last 10 years, it's gotten fantastic.

537
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:22,400
Another great stride in mining is currently taking place in Australia.

538
00:42:23,760 --> 00:42:29,140
The government-funded Commonwealth Scientific and Research Organization has retrofitted

539
00:42:29,141 --> 00:42:34,500
a load-haul dump machine to operate as an almost 100% autonomous robot.

540
00:42:34,501 --> 00:42:41,500
Seen here in early test footage, the LHD is programmed to enter a mine and report to a

541
00:42:41,501 --> 00:42:46,500
particular location without human intervention.

542
00:42:46,501 --> 00:42:51,500
The machine navigates through drifts and stopes using two laser scanners made by the

543
00:42:51,501 --> 00:42:56,500
SICK Corporation, one on the front and one on the rear.

544
00:42:56,501 --> 00:43:02,500
The devices scan in 180-degree arcs, looking for specific landmarks that have been programmed

545
00:43:02,501 --> 00:43:03,800
into an onboard computer.

546
00:43:05,080 --> 00:43:07,660
Inside the device, a laser reflects off a mirror

547
00:43:07,661 --> 00:43:09,900
that rotates 75 times a second.

548
00:43:11,460 --> 00:43:14,140
The laser bounces off the surrounding environment

549
00:43:14,141 --> 00:43:16,020
and returns to the device,

550
00:43:16,460 --> 00:43:18,940
indicating distances and topographic features.

551
00:43:19,780 --> 00:43:21,480
The data are then sent to the computer,

552
00:43:22,120 --> 00:43:24,060
and the computer operates the steering,

553
00:43:24,380 --> 00:43:26,260
acceleration, and braking controls.

554
00:43:28,680 --> 00:43:31,580
Autonomous LHDs reduce the number of miners needed

555
00:43:31,581 --> 00:43:33,660
and therefore cut mining costs.

556
00:43:34,580 --> 00:43:40,420
If there are ways to produce an ounce of silver that cost less money,

557
00:43:41,060 --> 00:43:43,000
that way is going to be used.

558
00:43:43,180 --> 00:43:48,020
So robotics may very well have a major role in the future of mining.

559
00:43:48,760 --> 00:43:50,520
Until that future comes,

560
00:43:51,180 --> 00:43:53,420
mining will continue to be done the traditional way,

561
00:43:53,421 --> 00:43:59,100
with muscle, might, machines, and miners.

